No. With placement, each group runs on its own server (or cluster) with only its own code and dependencies, and the groups still work together as one project.

What a server receives:

- the project's global `requirements.txt`, which every function needs;
- the `requirements.txt` from the folder of each function placed on it (`functions/<name>/requirements.txt`);
- none of the folders of functions placed elsewhere, so they are left out of its bundle.

So it is close to the per-function package you know, except that functions in the same group share one server.

A group holds:

- exactly one of `server`, `cluster` or `local: true`, except the default group, which may leave all three out and fall back to `options.deploy` / `--server`;
- `functions`, the list of functions it runs;
- optionally `port`, needed when two groups share a server;
- optionally `advertise`, the URL the other groups call when the server's own address is not reachable.

Functions not listed anywhere, and every workflow, belong to the default group. A useful negative case for your test is a group with both `server` and `cluster`.
